A||||4||kiteman||Help on Installing Sunroof Gasket||||5||
Z||000000||kiteman||08-30-2001||09:34 PM||||Anyone have experience installing a sunroof gasket?  I have an '84 SE which leaks water when it rains.  I changed all the grommets on the attachment hardware, but it didn't solve the problem, even though they were deteriorated.  The gasket has a pucker in one corner, where the sunroof probably wasn't closed properly and left for a long period.  It now has a permanent set, and I can see residue in that area after it rains.<P>I looked the job up in the archives.  Ogre says to pretty much rip out the old gasket, clean up the surface, apply door gasket adhesive, and glue the new gasket back on.<P>Any special tricks?  Things to watch out for?  I'd appreciate any help I can get!||||reg||5
Z||000001||Rodrv6||08-30-2001||10:06 PM||||There is a GM service bulletin to drill some drain holes to help keep the water out.  After you remove the gasket, carefully drill a 1/4 inch hole at each corner right under where the inner edge of the gasket will hide it when installed.  Drill ONLY through the fiberglass outer roof panel.  The steel subframe roof panel underneath already has drain channels built in from the factory.  Then install the new gasket with some weatherstrip adhesive in the channel.  I've done this on my 84 and now I'm nice and dry  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sunroof1a_4.JPG">  <P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Rodrv6 (edited 08-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||kiteman||08-31-2001||07:02 PM||||Thanks.  I really appreciate the info and the diagram.  Getting 330K miles from your '84 is awesome.  How many engine rebuilds have you done on it?||||reg||7
Z||000003||Rodrv6||08-31-2001||10:48 PM||||Well.....<BR>Pontiac was kind enough to give me a new engine at 99,600 miles as part of a recall, so the engine itself is "only" 230,000 miles old.  I've only done one rebuild on it about 70,000 miles ago, but I've had it apart at least partially several times to replace broken camshaft timing gears.  A set of all steel gears should have solved that problem (I hope!).<BR>About 3 years ago I totally dissassembled the entire car and rebuilt it with new stuff as needed.  Mostly suspension pieces and a new battery tray.  The rest of it was in good shape.  I still have it, although it is not a daily driver anymore, especially since I got an 88 GT early this year.  It's a LOT of fun to drive.  I guess I'm hooked on Fiero's  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||7
Z||000004||kiteman||09-01-2001||08:32 AM||||I removed the old gasket last night.  It came out pretty easily.  The factory applied LOTS of adhesive in the corners, and only a little in the channels, mostly under the edges.  I cleaned up the corners and drilled the holes, but getting the thin strip of adhesive off under the edges of the channels is nearly impossible without damaging the surface.<P>Any recommendations on reapplying adhesive?  Can I leave what's underneath the edges of the channels on, and just apply new.  It's still bonded pretty tightly. If I have to remove it, does anyone have any tips?  Do I apply lots of adhesive in the corners, as Pontiac did?||||reg||5
